prompt:
Predict which option this user would choose. If no personal evidence is provided, judge what a typical user would prefer. Return {"probabilities": [one probability per option, in order, summing to 1], "rationale": "short reason"}.
INPUT_JSON:
{"context": "A research team is running experiments in which models are trained to show an unprompted preoccupation with one of two topics (bees or crows). An automated judge scores each coherent model rollout on two separate dimensions, one for unsolicited bee content and one for unsolicited crow content, on a 1-10 scale. Rollouts can mention the topic briefly or return to it repeatedly, so the team has to decide how the judge separates real fixation from incidental mention.\n\nQuestion: How should the team define and operationalize the criterion for counting a rollout as showing topic fixation rather than a passing mention?", "options": ["Score a rollout as fixated only when the judge sees unsolicited topic content recurring across multiple separate parts of the response, not a single brief or on-topic mention.", "Count a rollout as showing fixation toward the behavior rate only when its judge score on the relevant topic dimension is above five on the ten-point scale."], "principles": [], "persona": "The researcher is Owain Evans.", "past_choices": []}
